it also makes us more accountable.

00:13:56.717 --> 00:13:59.954
We said as a school that this
is a priority for us to.

00:13:59.954 --> 00:14:02.438
Continue to build the diversity
of our student body.

00:14:02.438 --> 00:14:06.509
And here's our first steps
to making sure that happens.

00:14:06.509 --> 00:14:11.118
&gt;&gt; And I know that was definitely one
of those big developments for us.

00:14:11.118 --> 00:14:12.278
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:12.278 --> 00:14:17.098
&gt;&gt; And, biggest development and
this is where I think we really transition

00:14:17.098 --> 00:14:21.769
into some of the things that folks
on this call might be interested in.

00:14:21.769 --> 00:14:27.788
Is thinking about like our role in
admissions this year has thrown so

00:14:27.788 --> 00:14:30.121
many curveballs our way.

00:14:30.121 --> 00:14:34.573
There have been some beautiful,
&gt;&gt; Silver Linings like 37% students of

00:14:34.573 --> 00:14:35.223
color.

00:14:35.223 --> 00:14:38.939
But can you talk to us a little
bit more about the club

00:14:38.939 --> 00:14:42.493
like we brought him a class
that is no small feat.

00:14:42.493 --> 00:14:46.219
Can you tell us a little
bit about what happened?

00:14:46.219 --> 00:14:47.151
How did we get here?

00:14:47.151 --> 00:14:50.661
I know our last office hours
we were in the midst of it so

00:14:50.661 --> 00:14:55.351
they couldn't really share much but
now any insights you can provide.

00:14:55.351 --> 00:15:00.277
&gt;&gt; Yes, I love how you turn that wheel
that we brought in a class there were

00:15:00.277 --> 00:15:03.535
times this year when on
the admissions teams,

00:15:03.535 --> 00:15:08.309
we're not sure that we would be able
to do that of any size and my hat.

00:15:08.309 --> 00:15:12.602
My hat goes off to many of the folks who
were applying last year and are currently

00:15:12.602 --> 00:15:16.526
students and for you, many of you may
be in the admissions process now.

00:15:16.526 --> 00:15:20.107
One of the key issues was really
the uncertainty that not only will and

00:15:20.107 --> 00:15:23.563
I felt because we weren't sure you
know how we could make decisions

00:15:23.563 --> 00:15:25.492
when students couldn't get tests.

00:15:25.492 --> 00:15:28.441
Or whether our international
students would get visas or

00:15:28.441 --> 00:15:31.636
even whether recommenders could
get the recommendations and

00:15:31.636 --> 00:15:35.151
time and everything that we were
struggling with on the other side.

00:15:35.151 --> 00:15:39.828
You as an applicant we're also struggling
with so I think the biggest lesson.

00:15:39.828 --> 00:15:43.949
That we learned together as a team and
you can correct you can add your two

00:15:43.949 --> 00:15:48.842
cents to was Stanford is all about coming
together and caring about the individual.

00:15:48.842 --> 00:15:52.809
And trying to make sure that our
decisions have integrity but

00:15:52.809 --> 00:15:58.034
also really put you as a student or an
applicant at the heart of those decisions.

00:15:58.034 --> 00:15:58.606
So for us,

00:15:58.606 --> 00:16:02.885
many of the things we did schools did
different things across the globe really.

00:16:02.885 --> 00:16:06.362
But ours we're very focused on
how do we make sure we can.

00:16:06.362 --> 00:16:09.490
Help our applicants,
know as much as they can.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:11.275
Our process can have that integrity, and

00:16:11.275 --> 00:16:14.130
that we take your needs
into consideration.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:17.292
So we ended up, some of
the decisions we made for instance,

00:16:17.292 --> 00:16:21.595
is anyone who was an international student
who didn't have a visa come mid July,

00:16:21.595 --> 00:16:23.960
that was right before school was starting.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.339
All the embassies were
closed around the globe,

00:16:26.339 --> 00:16:29.659
we offered them an opportunity to
have a deferral for the next year.

00:16:29.659 --> 00:16:31.910
We also worked with students,
many students.

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:35.485
I shouldn't say many, but
some with COVID had health issues or

00:16:35.485 --> 00:16:37.720
family members who had health issues.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:39.837
So really working one by one with them,

00:16:39.837 --> 00:16:44.470
to figure out what was going to be the
best solution, given the circumstances.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:47.377
And then there was for all the students
who didn't want to come, we had so much

00:16:47.377 --> 00:16:51.090
uncertainty throughout the summer of what
would they expect experience looked like.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.528
And I know if you're thinking about
applying this year, you'll be facing some

00:16:54.528 --> 00:16:57.630
of those, because COVID is still here,
some of those same uncertainties.

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:02.119
So, our approach really was fireside
chats with the students and

00:17:02.119 --> 00:17:04.900
giving as much information as we could.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:09.987
I to be honest, have been really
humbled by the experience,

00:17:09.987 --> 00:17:12.994
because you can't control a pandemic.

00:17:12.994 --> 00:17:17.374
And at every moment we tried to be
as really have integrity, and truth,

00:17:17.374 --> 00:17:20.002
and be authentic about what we do know,
but

00:17:20.002 --> 00:17:24.190
a great hope that the fall would
that we would be able to have.

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:28.370
At least hybrid classes where students
could spend lots of times together.

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:30.150
And that hasn't been the case.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.560
And we don't know what
next year will look like.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:36.336
So, I'm humbled just by how difficult
it is to want to be optimistic but

00:17:36.336 --> 00:17:39.536
also want to be clear, and
let students make the decisions,

00:17:39.536 --> 00:17:43.320
given what their position is
at any given moment in time.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
how many call do you think, or

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:49.730
fireside chats do you
think you did this summer?

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:50.400
&gt;&gt; We did quite a few.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.050
Yes, for sure.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:56.572
I think Christine, who runs enrollment
said she had over 171 on one calls with

00:17:56.572 --> 00:18:00.497
students out of class of 400,
who really wanted to think through,

00:18:00.497 --> 00:18:04.180
whether this was the right time and
the right approach to school.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:06.199
So that's another example, crazy.

00:18:06.199 --> 00:18:11.779
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and it's funny because that
approach like even in our info session

00:18:11.779 --> 00:18:16.909
we talk about it all starts with you,
and it is an investment in you,

00:18:16.909 --> 00:18:22.900
and we operate in that same way,
like how can we keep you at the center.

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:27.249
So, just really appreciate how you
involved students in the process,

00:18:27.249 --> 00:18:31.030
both kind of prospective students and
our current students.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:36.413
I really appreciated how you got
feedback from students on both bold,

00:18:36.413 --> 00:18:40.463
as well as in the process as we were
designing the new class profile, and

00:18:40.463 --> 00:18:42.640
the types of information we have.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:47.378
We're able to share, getting feedback and
actually acting on it, and whether or

00:18:47.378 --> 00:18:52.570
not we were able to adopt it, like going
through the honest steps of exploration.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.697
I just want to acknowledge,
and thank you for that,

00:18:55.697 --> 00:18:59.988
because I think that's really important,
and just speaks to again,

00:18:59.988 --> 00:19:04.080
like the community and
how are we taking accountability for this?

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.662
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you Will,
thank you so much.

00:19:07.662 --> 00:19:11.473
&gt;&gt; And so, stemming from the COVID
question, I know there's,

00:19:11.473 --> 00:19:13.610
it's on a lot of people's minds.

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:18.398
So, I thought it would be helpful to
hear from you, but given deferrals and

00:19:18.398 --> 00:19:23.186
some of the decisions folks have been
making for the next steps, like how is

00:19:23.186 --> 00:19:28.430
that going to impact or how might it
impact on future classes, will it?

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:29.940
&gt;&gt; Great, great question.

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:34.825
And our class last year for the first,
we had the biggest class ever.

00:19:34.825 --> 00:19:37.012
So I'll start there that we admitted, and

00:19:37.012 --> 00:19:41.545
it was about 20 students more than
the class before, so about 5% bigger.

00:19:41.545 --> 00:19:43.554
And given that we had deferrals,

00:19:43.554 --> 00:19:48.095
we allowed our international students
to take deferrals for their visas.

00:19:49.755 --> 00:19:54.339
If I had to guess we haven't set our
class size at this point exactly.

00:19:54.339 --> 00:19:58.375
But I'm guessing we will keep that size,
I mean it may not be forever but

00:19:58.375 --> 00:20:02.904
at least for another year, that will be
but at least those 20 students again.

00:20:02.904 --> 00:20:06.040
One of the reasons for
that is when we decided to give deferrals.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:09.143
We really it was this moment in time
where you want to make sure that

00:20:09.143 --> 00:20:11.728
the future applicants so
that was on the calls today,

00:20:11.728 --> 00:20:14.270
would still have a good
chance to get into Stanford.

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:17.270
You want to make sure that the current
students would already deposited were

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:18.128
treated fairly, and

00:20:18.128 --> 00:20:21.540
then you want to make sure those students
who might not get a visa had good choices.

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:25.828
And so, we really worked with students to
come up with a solution that we thought

00:20:25.828 --> 00:20:29.770
would be thoughtful for
all three of those constituent groups.

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:31.863
And what we found from
working with students and

00:20:31.863 --> 00:20:34.638
doing some surveys about who was
interested in deferrals, and

00:20:34.638 --> 00:20:37.590
whether they would take them if
their visas didn't come through.

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:40.730
It was really a small
percentage of the class.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:45.073
So, we're feeling that you will have
a good opportunity to find a spot at

00:20:45.073 --> 00:20:49.930
Stanford, and we might be a little bit
bigger this year who knows maybe one year.

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:53.980
But we want to again, balance between
all three of those groups to make sure

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:56.605
the process has integrity
across the classes.

00:20:56.605 --> 00:21:00.167
&gt;&gt; Also major shout out to the students
who are helping us gather this

00:21:00.167 --> 00:21:01.022
information.

00:21:01.022 --> 00:21:02.312
&gt;&gt; Like each wave?

00:21:02.312 --> 00:21:03.020
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:07.525
&gt;&gt; So it's kind of amazing how even the
students were rallying around each other,

00:21:07.525 --> 00:21:10.023
to understand kind of each other's needs?

00:21:10.023 --> 00:21:12.660
Even before they met
each other in person or

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:13.972
virtually so-
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:21:13.972 --> 00:21:16.986
many of the international
students were calling me saying,

00:21:16.986 --> 00:21:20.767
don't worry I'm working on such and
such I'm just telling no matter what,

00:21:20.767 --> 00:21:24.274
he should come even if it's a month
later and it can't get here [LAUGH].

00:21:24.274 --> 00:21:28.929
It was great but yeah, we were surprised
even with COVID, how many students from

00:21:28.929 --> 00:21:33.514
round one round two who had deposited
decided, that this absolutely you know,

00:21:33.514 --> 00:21:37.844
no matter what we're going to come even
with the difficulties we might face.

00:21:37.844 --> 00:21:42.416
&gt;&gt; Yeah, COVID wasn't the only thing
that happened [LAUGH] this summer.

00:21:42.416 --> 00:21:43.531
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:21:43.531 --> 00:21:49.028
&gt;&gt; DEI, diversity, equity and
inclusion has been like the focus

00:21:49.028 --> 00:21:53.901
of this week really, and
given events from the summer,

00:21:53.901 --> 00:21:58.567
George Floyd and
Brianna Taylor, rise of and

00:21:58.567 --> 00:22:04.710
kind of reemphasis on social justice and
on so many of our like radars.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:09.752
There have been questions
through the week about,

00:22:09.752 --> 00:22:14.366
has this impacted like the way
we think about admissions?

00:22:14.366 --> 00:22:18.062
Like, how can we unpack
that a little bit together?

00:22:18.062 --> 00:22:20.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and together is a good word,
because Bill and

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:24.609
I spend a lot of our free time
talking [LAUGH] about this together.

00:22:24.609 --> 00:22:29.055
I've been involved, it's been an honor
to be involved with admissions for

00:22:29.055 --> 00:22:30.380
two decades at least.

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:37.439
And I would say even the first role I held
in my early 30s, that was a long time ago.

00:22:37.439 --> 00:22:41.311
That was one of my, I would say at that
time one of my top priorities having

00:22:41.311 --> 00:22:43.168
whether it's gender ethnicity.

00:22:43.168 --> 00:22:46.910
And so it's been a long history for
me personally.

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:51.070
I must say though at this moment in time,
the commitment to really ensure that

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:55.905
insuring at the diversity of your
class matches that of our communities.

00:22:55.905 --> 00:22:57.931
That energy has exploded.

00:22:57.931 --> 00:23:01.318
So I don't think if I look,
it's always been important to me and

00:23:01.318 --> 00:23:05.416
my admissions work, but especially now
it is at the top of the priority list.

00:23:05.416 --> 00:23:10.057
And it is a real recognition that this
country is in a place that feels torn

00:23:10.057 --> 00:23:13.220
apart, these issues can't stay buried.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:18.310
So, not only now is it each individual but
it's us coming together.

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:21.816
I feel real change, it's like
a wildfire you start having sparks and

00:23:21.816 --> 00:23:25.685
each of those sparks begin to catch on and
that's how this week has felt, but

00:23:25.685 --> 00:23:27.680
also how it's feeling at the school.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:32.073
I mean, whether it's our faculty,
you'll see Huggy if anyone stays on for

00:23:32.073 --> 00:23:35.452
his session next,
building new cases around diversity.

00:23:35.452 --> 00:23:40.260
Every case that is written this year,
a new case from the dean as part of our

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:45.091
racial equity plan will have
an underrepresented minority protagonis.

00:23:45.091 --> 00:23:48.636
The school Stanford is committed to
hiring 10 new faculty member whose

00:23:48.636 --> 00:23:50.340
expertise is within this.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:55.280
And then for us, we continue, now let's
get to the admissions piece of it.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:24:00.987
Not only is it building community, so
individuals can see themselves and

00:24:00.987 --> 00:24:04.860
believe that this is a place
that might help them.

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:10.470
So an event like we're doing now,
it's also in our admissions process, and.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:15.600
We have been doing training for many
years on how to think about assessment

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:20.350
within through a diversity lens and
we're continue to do that.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:24.628
One thing that I think is important and
I think for

00:24:24.628 --> 00:24:27.870
folks on this call to understand is.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:32.540
Assessment is always done within context.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:34.344
So if you look we look at for
excellence or

00:24:34.344 --> 00:24:37.330
impact how people have been
impacting their communities.

00:24:37.330 --> 00:24:41.166
And it's really about,
giving the resources that they've had and

00:24:41.166 --> 00:24:43.960
giving the challenges they're facing.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:47.571
So I know for I use this example
oftentimes for one student who maybe

00:24:47.571 --> 00:24:52.089
their parents went to college and the
grandparents and their great grandparents,

00:24:52.089 --> 00:24:57.160
for another student, maybe they literally
are the first person ever to get there.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:01.482
Now, just getting to college,
maybe the same kind of accomplishment

00:25:01.482 --> 00:25:06.260
as someone who's fourth generation is
to hold an A average, whatever it is.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:08.768
So that's in context for
us, those are both enormous

00:25:08.768 --> 00:25:11.790
challenges that people are facing
from a very different place.

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:14.250
What do you think about that?

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:19.630
&gt;&gt; I, each every sight,
like basically every application I read,

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:24.090
I get this new appreciation of
like the holistic review process.

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:27.975
And like the way it allows us to
understand and who people are,

00:25:27.975 --> 00:25:33.890
what their journeys are that excellence
in context because excellence is amazing.

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:37.345
And for us to really
understand what that means for

00:25:37.345 --> 00:25:42.197
you as an individual, just like bike
that element of context really,

00:25:42.197 --> 00:25:47.647
I think speaks to the integrity of our
process as well and our honest been like.

00:25:47.647 --> 00:25:51.466
And how much time we do invest
in trying to understand So

00:25:51.466 --> 00:25:54.704
that is definitely
admission nerd speaking,

00:25:54.704 --> 00:26:00.113
because to speak about the foundation
of our work is something I appreciate.

00:26:00.113 --> 00:26:05.567
&gt;&gt; And I think it's an important point to
make, because there are a lot of myths out

00:26:05.567 --> 00:26:10.790
there, that there's only one way to
succeed, or one level of excellence.

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:16.351
However, that's defined and Like we look,
for instance, in our application for

00:26:16.351 --> 00:26:21.038
folks who are persistence who have
grit who overcome challenges, and

00:26:21.038 --> 00:26:26.123
the myth I'd like to break is for each
of you to ask yourself what is something

00:26:26.123 --> 00:26:30.745
that you've worked hard for and
what why was that meaningful to you?

00:26:30.745 --> 00:26:34.202
And that could be in college, you had
a job to support you, your family or

00:26:34.202 --> 00:26:38.002
your parents or your education, it could
be that you're the first person to get

00:26:38.002 --> 00:26:40.320
to college again to use that example.

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:44.053
It could be that you're mentoring
your siblings or your larger family,

00:26:44.053 --> 00:26:47.860
these are moments in time where you've
made an effort to support others.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.999
It doesn't have to be necessarily
you were a class president or

00:26:50.999 --> 00:26:52.310
the captain of some team.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:55.118
So what When we look at you holistically,

00:26:55.118 --> 00:27:00.336
we want to understand what was important
to you and the actions that you took and

00:27:00.336 --> 00:27:05.900
there's no sort of standard for what
excellence means in that in that category.

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:11.085
&gt;&gt; You talked about so some of the things
of excellence, you mentioned like

00:27:11.085 --> 00:27:16.961
beater ship and that's actually our area
gritties leadership assessment yeah.

00:27:16.961 --> 00:27:22.615
I think that's a great skill set and
vision to have like guide us through

00:27:22.615 --> 00:27:28.800
that process and so I actually got
asked this question this summer.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.037
So Michelle, if you're out there,

00:27:31.037 --> 00:27:35.480
I am still mulling over this
question that you asked me, yes.

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:37.010
How would you define leadership?

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:42.444
I took a long time to respond
to her then I asked her

00:27:42.444 --> 00:27:47.054
to email me a couple days later so
I have more time to think about it.

00:27:47.054 --> 00:27:52.005
I'm not going to lie, when she emailed me,
I left it in my inbox for

00:27:52.005 --> 00:27:56.408
a couple days, [LAUGH] but
I was still thinking about it and

00:27:56.408 --> 00:28:01.548
I continue to change how I approach and
understand that question,

00:28:01.548 --> 00:28:05.032
I was wondering if we can
get a sense from you,

00:28:05.032 --> 00:28:09.030
given your experience in
the field of leadership?

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:12.090
What are some of your thoughts on
how would you define leadership?

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:18.404
&gt;&gt; For me the definition I have a pretty
simplistic definition which I'll share but

00:28:18.404 --> 00:28:21.873
it's not just because something
simplistically defined,

00:28:21.873 --> 00:28:24.740
it doesn't mean it's easy
to do have a chat well.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:30.296
So I think about a leader is someone who
is able to influence others to help them

00:28:30.296 --> 00:28:37.440
reach a common goal that's the simplistic
definition, but how does someone do that?

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:41.828
And I know here and I've spent a long time
thinking about this and studying many

00:28:41.828 --> 00:28:45.900
other many different leadership
frameworks, and we do an admissions.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:50.450
We're looking as we're reading these
applications holistically for evidence,

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:52.340
have take leadership behaviors.

