00:43:38.519 --> 00:43:42.594
Well when I worked at the NBA
this is what it looked like,

00:43:42.594 --> 00:43:45.293
that's how you have some dialogue.

00:43:45.293 --> 00:43:49.550
But it's also to industry we
look at what your function is.

00:43:49.550 --> 00:43:53.116
So it's wonderful to have someone in the
room who's actually had to sell something,

00:43:53.116 --> 00:43:55.410
who's had to build something,
to have the engineer.

00:43:55.410 --> 00:44:00.476
We have doctors and lawyers, we have a
pastor, we have a professional violinist.

00:44:00.476 --> 00:44:04.496
[LAUGH] So there are many different
ways that they can bring their point of

00:44:04.496 --> 00:44:08.051
view to solving different problems
that are in the classroom.

00:44:08.051 --> 00:44:13.036
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and it comes back to anchoring in
yourself and the perspective you bring.

00:44:13.036 --> 00:44:16.106
Dean Levon actually opened leadership,

00:44:16.106 --> 00:44:19.111
well I kind of stole his
thunder because I quoted him.

00:44:19.111 --> 00:44:20.942
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; because I didn't realize what he

00:44:20.942 --> 00:44:21.923
was going to talk about.

00:44:21.923 --> 00:44:27.563
He spoke about education fundamentally
being about engaging and encountering

00:44:27.563 --> 00:44:33.420
difference, and how that kind of has you
challenge kind of your own worldview.

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:37.851
And even if you don't have to accept or
agree with everything, these are different

00:44:37.851 --> 00:44:42.320
ways for you to continue pushing
the boundaries of your understanding.

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:46.703
So it's kind of nice how this
does come full circle, and

00:44:46.703 --> 00:44:51.554
how diversity really is a critical
part of both our identity as

00:44:51.554 --> 00:44:56.990
an institution and how we want to
keep evolving over time, so.

00:44:56.990 --> 00:45:02.195
&gt;&gt; I want one other thing since we're
talking about applications, thought Will,

00:45:02.195 --> 00:45:06.481
is another misconception I believe
is that you are your industry,

00:45:06.481 --> 00:45:07.874
if that makes sense.

00:45:07.874 --> 00:45:12.607
That somehow we're reading all the energy
or all the education or the healthcare,

00:45:12.607 --> 00:45:17.540
and that so much of your application is
because you have learned about this thing.

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:22.599
What I found fascinating over a couple
decades is I can read 15 different

00:45:22.599 --> 00:45:27.594
people who are doing say private equity or
consulting, whatever it is.

00:45:27.594 --> 00:45:29.938
And the way that they show leadership or

00:45:29.938 --> 00:45:33.640
the way that they've impacted
others varies dramatically.

00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:36.600
So this industry, it's not as if
it's the lens we're looking at you.

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:39.625
It's just one of those things
that are in your context, but

00:45:39.625 --> 00:45:42.360
please don't feel that
that's what you represent.

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:47.330
We're much more interested in what you
have done within your organization, and

00:45:47.330 --> 00:45:51.980
that even the same company can
be dramatically different.

00:45:51.980 --> 00:45:52.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:54.145 --> 00:45:56.620
There's a question here from Hiskey.

00:45:56.620 --> 00:45:59.910
And this is a question that really speaks
kind of to a lot of the work I do.

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:03.822
So that's why I'm kind of
bias towards this question.

00:46:03.822 --> 00:46:08.514
What are some future initiatives that
the mission team has in the pipeline and

00:46:08.514 --> 00:46:10.340
is excited about?

00:46:10.340 --> 00:46:12.200
&gt;&gt; Well Will,
why don't you start with that one then?

00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:13.810
[LAUGH] I'd love to toss it to you.

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:18.829
&gt;&gt; I'm really excited about not
just expansion of audiences and

00:46:18.829 --> 00:46:22.130
trying to really help folks
see themselves at the GSB.

00:46:22.130 --> 00:46:27.351
But how can we also leverage the work
we do, the reach that we do have,

00:46:27.351 --> 00:46:33.400
to help people put their best foot forward
regardless of what they end up doing.

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:36.979
So they might apply somewhere else,
they might decide to stay in industry.

00:46:36.979 --> 00:46:41.961
But in our outreach efforts can we
do some of the things that kind

00:46:41.961 --> 00:46:47.060
of challenge you to have that
audacity to believe in yourself.

00:46:47.060 --> 00:46:48.954
&gt;&gt; Aah, love it.
&gt;&gt; Or understand yourself,

00:46:48.954 --> 00:46:52.031
these new ways so that you can-
&gt;&gt; I love it.

00:46:52.031 --> 00:46:54.639
&gt;&gt; Have that creative confidence
to see something through or

00:46:54.639 --> 00:46:56.740
try something different.

00:46:56.740 --> 00:47:02.427
And so while it's not necessarily a
tangible, singular thing that we're doing,

00:47:02.427 --> 00:47:07.318
I think it's almost that philosophy
that under the kind of the spirit.

00:47:07.318 --> 00:47:12.007
And actually that was a lot of what
I hoped to develop with diversity and

00:47:12.007 --> 00:47:16.540
leadership this week, to kind of it
not just be about admissions but

00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:20.060
are there things that
people can take away?

00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:24.156
Whether it is realizations about
themselves or maybe small actions they can

00:47:24.156 --> 00:47:28.710
do in their everyday lives around the
topic of diversity, equity, and inclusion.

00:47:28.710 --> 00:47:33.515
So super thank you for
posing that question.

00:47:33.515 --> 00:47:34.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's awesome.

00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:37.605
&gt;&gt; This is something that is so
meaningful for me, and

00:47:37.605 --> 00:47:40.510
this is almost like my what matters.

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:41.750
&gt;&gt; That is great.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:42.522
I love it, Will.

00:47:42.522 --> 00:47:46.251
And I think it really captures,
I'm lighting up because it captures,

00:47:46.251 --> 00:47:49.914
if you think about our presentation,
even from the last decade,

00:47:49.914 --> 00:47:53.190
it's not here your classes,
here's what the clubs are.

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:55.830
It's really who are you and
what do you want to do?

00:47:55.830 --> 00:47:58.336
And I think I share, Will,
with you so I aligned so

00:47:58.336 --> 00:48:01.802
closely that our hope is that we're
helping you get in touch with what

00:48:01.802 --> 00:48:04.530
you want to accomplish no
matter where you apply.

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:08.687
And certainly Stanford may not be
a good fit for every individual, so

00:48:08.687 --> 00:48:13.345
it's great to be an admissions role where
your true purpose is helping people

00:48:13.345 --> 00:48:18.098
reach their potential no matter where it's
going to be the right place for them.

00:48:18.098 --> 00:48:20.857
So so well put, Will, thank you.

00:48:20.857 --> 00:48:24.939
&gt;&gt; You could tell I get really
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:24.939 --> 00:48:25.950
&gt;&gt; Again, admission nerd.

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:26.950
&gt;&gt; Stanford, yeah.

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:28.157
That's awesome.
&gt;&gt; So this is going to put

00:48:28.157 --> 00:48:29.717
you in a hot seat, Kirsten.

00:48:29.717 --> 00:48:31.075
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:48:31.075 --> 00:48:36.237
&gt;&gt; And this is a question from And again
to everybody out there, your answer is

00:48:36.237 --> 00:48:41.345
your own and celebrate that, but we're
curious with Kristen in the here and now.

00:48:41.345 --> 00:48:42.595
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:48:42.595 --> 00:48:44.440
&gt;&gt; And what matters to you, why?

00:48:45.850 --> 00:48:52.400
&gt;&gt; It's so funny that I didn't even see
this question on the list, but I love it.

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:57.795
It's very close well to what you said,
my whole career if I could look back and

00:48:57.795 --> 00:49:01.620
connect the dots has been
about trying to help people.

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:06.612
Essentially, know what's
important to them and

00:49:06.612 --> 00:49:11.260
have the courage to step in and
expand what they think they're capable of.

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:14.757
And I've been really lucky to not
only be doing admissions work,

00:49:14.757 --> 00:49:18.649
but also had a chance at Stanford for
many years to work in the classroom.

00:49:18.649 --> 00:49:19.469
And of course,

00:49:19.469 --> 00:49:23.650
we call interpersonal dynamics to help
people with their leadership skills.

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:27.893
After I left Stanford before
I came back again as Dean,

00:49:27.893 --> 00:49:31.538
I was working in the private sector
in an executive search firm,

00:49:31.538 --> 00:49:34.320
doing lots of coaching
with senior executives.

00:49:34.320 --> 00:49:37.730
So it's so much a part of who I am.

00:49:37.730 --> 00:49:42.021
And in this role, it's a wonderful
platform to be speaking with

00:49:42.021 --> 00:49:46.072
applicants who are early in their careers,
almost every day

00:49:46.072 --> 00:49:50.777
about what they want to accomplish and
how we could help them get there.

00:49:50.777 --> 00:49:53.807
And that really is what's
most important to me.

00:49:53.807 --> 00:49:56.521
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
I know that can often be a really

00:49:56.521 --> 00:49:57.773
question.

00:49:57.773 --> 00:49:59.025
&gt;&gt; I know it kind of sucks.

00:49:59.025 --> 00:50:02.266
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:50:02.266 --> 00:50:03.013
&gt;&gt; I know it's hard to answer that

00:50:03.013 --> 00:50:04.343
question on this-
&gt;&gt; No.

00:50:04.343 --> 00:50:07.889
&gt;&gt; But actually, thank you for
embracing that question.

00:50:07.889 --> 00:50:09.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:09.755 --> 00:50:13.645
&gt;&gt; And I think that's just something
fun to hear from the leader

00:50:13.645 --> 00:50:18.950
of our admissions team to hear what
some of your guiding principles are.

00:50:18.950 --> 00:50:21.160
&gt;&gt; I'll say one more thing about it too.

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:25.860
I feel on this role different
than other times in my career.

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.170
It's an enormous responsibility.

00:50:28.170 --> 00:50:31.983
Because if at the heart, you're trying
to help people develop and we have

00:50:31.983 --> 00:50:35.918
an opportunity to come to a place that
Will and I both deeply believe in making

00:50:35.918 --> 00:50:40.177
those decisions thoughtfully and trying
to do them with fairness and integrity.

00:50:40.177 --> 00:50:44.065
And trying to bring in so many, as we
said, diverse perspectives and class.

00:50:44.065 --> 00:50:47.385
It's a lot of responsibility to do
that right for the institution and

00:50:47.385 --> 00:50:49.617
do it right for
everybody who's on this call,

00:50:49.617 --> 00:50:53.298
to treat each of you with the dignity and
respect you deserve in the process.

00:50:53.298 --> 00:50:57.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we are starting to
creep up to the top of the hour.

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:02.310
If you could have any parting
words of wisdom advice,

00:51:02.310 --> 00:51:05.278
something that you hope
folks can take home

00:51:05.278 --> 00:51:09.740
with them after today's session Kristen,
what would that be?

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:15.634
&gt;&gt; The top card is believe in yourself,
invest in yourself,

00:51:15.634 --> 00:51:18.730
but may not be the NBA
that's the next step.

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:20.471
But challenge your dreaming and

00:51:20.471 --> 00:51:25.230
your visualization from all of
the wonderful stories you've heard about.

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:26.910
How will you step into your own power?

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:29.620
How will you be brave because we need you?

00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:31.080
The world needs you.

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:31.804
So that's my top card.

00:51:31.804 --> 00:51:36.769
And then the second card, and I'm feeling
this much more in the last month as

00:51:36.769 --> 00:51:40.339
COVID continues,
the pandemic continues to heat up,

00:51:40.339 --> 00:51:45.090
is be thoughtful about,
know that this is a difficult time.

00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:49.823
And for you as an individual right now,
think about your current situation, what

00:51:49.823 --> 00:51:54.693
you're learning, how much you can learn,
how whether it's social justice or it is

00:51:54.693 --> 00:51:59.649
the pandemic is affecting that runway that
you have over the next year to 18 months.

00:52:00.690 --> 00:52:03.230
because it is a difficult time for
everyone.

00:52:03.230 --> 00:52:05.162
And whether if you want to
come to business school,

00:52:05.162 --> 00:52:06.710
it's not like it was two years ago.

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:09.772
Probably your job is not
exactly like that as well, so

00:52:09.772 --> 00:52:12.915
just take some time to think for
such a big investment.

00:52:12.915 --> 00:52:16.266
And what in the world isn't
the uncertainty, what's right for you?

00:52:16.266 --> 00:52:19.000
What does that trade off look like?

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:23.521
And hopefully, you can get some
good advice from mentors around and

00:52:23.521 --> 00:52:28.770
folks like us on our team to help you get
a greater clarity about that decision.

00:52:28.770 --> 00:52:31.010
But at the end of the day,
there's no perfect choice.

00:52:32.650 --> 00:52:33.862
&gt;&gt; Thank you for those words, Kristen.

00:52:33.862 --> 00:52:35.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:52:35.150 --> 00:52:38.917
&gt;&gt; And so with that, to everybody
who has joined us, thank you so

00:52:38.917 --> 00:52:41.023
much over the course of this week.

00:52:41.023 --> 00:52:46.025
I hope you've been able to not just get
a sense for who we are at Stanford,

00:52:46.025 --> 00:52:50.699
but I hope you're able to also get
some insights into the process and

00:52:50.699 --> 00:52:53.580
hopefully insights into yourself.

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:58.460
As you're able to unpack and untangle
all of the different things that make

00:52:58.460 --> 00:53:03.184
you who you are, I really hope that
also provides some of those lenses for

00:53:03.184 --> 00:53:07.909
you to really appreciate and
understand those around you with empathy,

00:53:07.909 --> 00:53:10.249
thoughtfulness, and curiosity.

00:53:10.249 --> 00:53:14.474
Because that's the way that we're really
going to be able to get out there and

00:53:14.474 --> 00:53:18.179
solve the world's problems working
together across difference,

00:53:18.179 --> 00:53:19.999
celebrating that difference and

00:53:19.999 --> 00:53:23.720
realizing the potential that
diverse perspectives have to offer.

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:29.432
So with that, this is going to be
the last time this bubblehead will be-

00:53:29.432 --> 00:53:29.944
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:53:29.944 --> 00:53:31.335
&gt;&gt; Signing off.

00:53:31.335 --> 00:53:35.241
I wannna thank you again,
we have one more event for Diversity and

00:53:35.241 --> 00:53:38.515
Leadership 2020 Connect With Community.

00:53:38.515 --> 00:53:42.615
This evening, Professor Huggy Rao
will be doing one more case study and

00:53:42.615 --> 00:53:44.890
featuring a very special guest.

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:48.940
So while I'll be behind the scenes,
this is going to be my goodbye to you.

00:53:48.940 --> 00:53:51.470
Hopefully, we'll cross paths again soon.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:54.184
And until then, take care, stay safe and

00:53:54.184 --> 00:53:57.456
keep asking yourself what
matters to you and why.

00:53:57.456 --> 00:53:59.270
